{
  "label": "not_comparable",
  "factor": "other",
  "rationale": "Both claims address change from baseline in SBP with semaglutide in T2D, but the contrasts are not the same. Side A (PMID 29764222, Bayesian NMA) compares once-weekly subcutaneous semaglutide 1.0 mg against SGLT-2 inhibitors and reports only that 'SBP reduction was not statistically differentiable' (sentence 10), with no point estimate. Side B (PMID 38097902, meta-analysis) compares oral semaglutide against placebo or other antihyperglycemic agents and reports a significant WMD of -2.50 mmHg (95% CI: -3.48 to -1.53) in the oral-administration subgroup (sentence 9). Two essential elements differ: (1) the semaglutide formulation/dose (once-weekly subcutaneous 1.0 mg vs oral), and (2) decisively, the reference treatment - SGLT-2 inhibitors, which themselves lower blood pressure, versus placebo or other AHAs. A null difference versus SGLT-2i and a significant reduction versus placebo/AHA answer different questions and do not constitute an identifiable comparable contrast, so the findings cannot be aligned. Diagnostic note (not established as material error): side A's assertion is labelled 'explicit_no_difference', but 'not statistically differentiable' is statistical-evidence wording rather than a substantive no-effect conclusion; this does not change the comparability obstacle, which is decisive here.",
  "diagnostics": {
    "material_extraction_error": false,
    "error_sides": [],
    "error_fields": [],
    "uncertain_extraction": true,
    "comparison_sufficient": false
  ,
  "source_evidence": [
    {
      "side": "a",
      "sentence_id": 10,
      "quote": "Once-weekly semaglutide 1.0 mg performed significantly better than all SGLT-2is of interest in reducing weight and improving FPG levels: however, SBP reduction was not statistically differentiable."
    },
    {
      "side": "a",
      "sentence_id": 2,
      "quote": "The present network meta-analysis (NMA) evaluated comparative efficacy and safety of once-weekly semaglutide and sodium-glucose co-transporter 2 inhibitors (SGLT-2is) in T2D patients inadequately controlled with metformin."
    },
    {
      "side": "b",
      "sentence_id": 9,
      "quote": "The reduction in SBP was similar for subcutaneous and oral administration of semaglutide, with -2.36 (95% CI: -3.38 to -1.35) and -2.50 (95% CI: -3.48 to -1.53), respectively."
    },
    {
      "side": "b",
      "sentence_id": 2,
      "quote": "Randomized controlled trials (RCTs) comparing subcutaneous or oral semaglutide with placebo or other antihyperglycemic agents (AHAs) in T2D patients were identified by searching PubMed, Embase, Web of Science, ClinicalTrials.gov and Cochrane Library."
    }
  ]
  ,
  "notes": "Source verification: side A claim faithfully reflects sentence 10 (SBP comparison vs SGLT-2is not statistically differentiable; no estimate reported, estimate_direction null is consistent). Side B claim faithfully reflects sentence 9 (oral-semaglutide subgroup WMD -2.50 mmHg, 95% CI -3.48 to -1.53, directional assertion). No material extraction error established on either side. The side A assertion label 'explicit_no_difference' rests on statistical wording ('not statistically differentiable') and should not be read as a substantive no-effect conclusion; this is recorded as an uncertainty rather than a confirmed error. Comparison: same outcome domain (SBP change from baseline) but non-matching index exposure (once-weekly subcutaneous semaglutide 1.0 mg vs oral semaglutide) and non-matching reference treatments (SGLT-2 inhibitors vs placebo/other AHAs), leaving no identifiable comparable contrast; hence not_comparable with factor 'other' (concrete comparator/formulation mismatch described in rationale)."
} }
